#v(3pt)
*Abstract.* Wilson-Gerow, Chen, and Stamp predict different photon-count probabilities for correlated worldline (CWL) and Schrödinger–Newton (SN) gravity in a two-pulse optomechanical experiment. We compare the first swap with both theories driven by the same laser envelope at the bare mechanical sideband. Reference [1] uses the shifted sideband in its CWL derivation. The CWL collective mechanical mode has the ordinary frequency, while each relative mode has the SN frequency. For the prescribed replica endpoints, the large-replica limit gives the relative-mode transition probabilities for both photon outcomes. Within the rotating-wave approximation, neither photon outcome distinguishes the models at this calibration. The SN/CWL zero-photon ratio is one, and the ideal zero-photon probability in each model is $pi^2 epsilon^4/4 + O(epsilon^6)$. We also identify a first-order discrepancy between two equations in the published derivation and quantify the limitation imposed by the counter-rotating interaction at a fixed pulse strength.

= What is held fixed
Wilson-Gerow, Chen, and Stamp [1] give the CWL zero-photon probability in their Eq. (126) and the SN probability in Eq. (138). For the first swap, their leading coefficients of $epsilon^4$ are $pi^2/(16e^2)$ and $pi^2/16$, respectively. The comparison turns on which oscillator defines the pulse area. A fractional change of order $epsilon^2$ in an area near perfect return creates a zero-photon probability of the very order being compared. The external position drive and the laser frequency therefore have to be specified before expanding that probability.

There is also a discrepancy within the CWL equations. Use the definition $h=epsilon^2=omega_"SN"^2/(2 Omega^2)$ given immediately before Eq. (87) of Ref. [1]. In Eq. (107) of Ref. [1] the term in $i dot(B)$ proportional to the optical mean $A$ is $g(1+h)A$. Averaging the individual mechanical equation (112) with the means in Eq. (110) instead gives $g(1+h/2)A$. For initial means $A=1$, $B=0$, the two equations give mechanical derivatives differing by $-i g h/2$. They use the same coordinates, before any final-state metric is applied. Equation (105) itself gives $(1-h) dot(B)=-i g A-i Omega h B$. Dividing this equation by $1-h$ recovers Eq. (107) to first order. Repairing this factor alone would leave unresolved how to implement both hypotheses at one laser setting.

Write $omega=omega_m$, $Omega^2=omega^2+omega_"SN"^2$ and $h=omega_"SN"^2/(2 Omega^2)$. We use units with $ℏ=1$. Let $F(t) q(a+a^dagger)$ be the linearized interaction, where $F(t)$ is the force coefficient of the prescribed classical intracavity pump. The operator $a$ describes the optical fluctuation about that pump and $q$ the displacement about the prescribed classical trajectory. We assume that residual one-mode drives have been removed. For a canonical oscillator of frequency $nu$,
$ q = (c+c^dagger)/sqrt(2m nu), quad g_nu(t)=F(t)/sqrt(2m nu), quad
  g_Omega/g_omega = (1-2h)^(1/4) = r. $
The last factor also appears in the journal's SN calculation, Eqs. (127)–(131). Choosing the bare sideband requires an independent value of $omega$, for example from the mass and bare stiffness. Tuning to a measured shifted resonance $Omega$ would select a different laser setting. We assume the bare reference, intracavity field and timing as inputs. Their experimental precision remains to be demonstrated.
#pagebreak()
= Two sectors of one pulse sequence
Take two same-phase rectangular pulses, each of duration $t_p=pi/(2g_*)$, at laser detuning $-omega$ relative to the cavity. The ordinary coupling during an ideal pulse is $g_*=omega/20$. The drive vanishes for a hold $T=t_p$ between pulses. The initial preparation is one cavity photon and the centered mechanical ground state of each model. There is no intermediate measurement. Small pulse offsets $d_i$ refer to the ordinary area, so that the two shifted-mode areas are $r(pi/2+d_i)$. Using the same symbol for the two models’ nominal areas without specifying $F$ would miss this shift.

For $N$ CWL replicas let $J$ be the all-ones matrix and $Pi=J/N$. From Eqs. (85)–(86) of Ref. [1], the squared-frequency matrix is $omega^2 Pi + Omega^2 (I-Pi)$. Rotate both the mechanical and optical coordinates by the same orthogonal matrix that diagonalizes $Pi$. The summed position interaction stays diagonal. The collective pair evolves with mechanical frequency $omega$ and coupling $g_omega$. Each relative pair evolves with frequency $Omega$ and coupling $g_Omega$. In the SN model joint parity of the centered initial state is preserved by the bilinear drive. The mechanical first moment consequently vanishes and the SN self-potential in Eq. (13) gives the oscillator at $Omega$. This argument uses the specified preparation throughout the unobserved pulse sequence.

In the frame rotating at $omega$, the number-conserving Hamiltonian in either sector is $H_nu=g_nu(t)X+(nu-omega)Q$. Here $X$ exchanges the optical and mechanical excitation, and $Q$ projects onto the mechanical excitation. Define $a_(0,nu)$ and $a_(1,nu)$ as the final mechanical and optical amplitudes, respectively, for optical input. For $delta=d_1+d_2$, $a_(0,omega)=i sin delta$ and $a_(1,omega)=-cos delta$. At perfect ordinary area, the excitation returns to the cavity exactly in this approximation.

To first order in $h$, $r=1-h/2+O(h^2)$ and $Omega-omega=omega h+O(omega h^2)$. The shift of the two areas gives $-i pi h/2$ in the final mechanical amplitude. The first-order detuning insertion is proportional to the time integral of $cos(pi-phi)sin phi$, where $phi(t)$ is the nominal accumulated ordinary pulse angle. The integrand vanishes on the hold. Reflection of the two ideal ramps about $phi=pi/2$ cancels their integrals. Consequently,
$ a_(0,Omega)=i(delta-pi h/2)+O(h^2), quad a_(1,Omega)=-1+O(h). $
These remainders are uniform for $abs(d_i)<=h/100$ at the fixed frequency ratio. For example, the integrated first-order perturbation norm is below $100h$ since $omega(2t_p+T)=30pi$. The integrated Taylor remainder is below $210h^2$ for $h<=0.01$. In the twice-iterated Duhamel formula the last propagator is the exact unitary. Its norm is one, so the ordered double integral contributes at most $(105h)^2/2$, in addition to $210h^2$. Thus $10^4h^2$ suffices for the displayed mechanical-amplitude error. At $delta=0$ this also checks the absolute SN coefficient against Ref. [1]. Its Eqs. (134) and (137) give total on-pulse area $2g t_p=pi sqrt(omega/Omega)=pi(1-h/2)+O(h^2)$ for $n=0$. With the detuning cancellation above, squaring the resulting amplitude gives $pi^2h^2/4+O(h^3)$, four times the coefficient printed in Eq. (138). An unknown $delta$ comparable with $h$ changes that coefficient; $delta=o(h)$ suffices to recover it.
