= Why the replica normalization matters
We use the exclusive replica-symmetric endpoints of Ref. [1]. The incoming optical Fock label is differentiated once in every replica. The two permitted readouts put the returned photon in every replica or the mechanical excitation in every replica. Ref. [1] first differentiates the coherent-state kernel once in every replica, takes the $N$th root of each squared endpoint amplitude, and then normalizes the two weights. For a photon count $j$ let $a_j=a_(j,Omega)$ be the relative amplitude and $u_j$ the collective amplitude including the outgoing endpoint metric. Equation (114) gives $u_1=a_(1,omega)$ and $u_0=sqrt(omega/Omega) a_(0,omega)$: the canonical collective annihilator is $sqrt(omega/Omega)$ times the paper's collective mechanical variable in Eq. (90). In particular $u_0=0$ at perfect ordinary area. The endpoint metric leaves this zero exact.

Returning to physical replica labels gives the matrix $a_j I+(u_j-a_j)Pi$. Differentiation gives its permanent. Write $z=(u_j-a_j)/a_j$ when $a_j != 0$. Choosing the $k$ columns contributed by $Pi$ leaves $k!$ permutations on those columns. With $(N)_k=N!/(N-k)!$, the endpoint amplitude obeys the finite identity
$ K_(j,N)/a_j^N = sum_(k=0)^N (N)_k (z/N)^k
   = integral_0^infinity e^(-t)(1+z t/N)^N dif t. $
The integral follows by expanding the binomial and integrating each power of $t$. At the ideal dark endpoint $z=-1$. The integral on $0<=t<=N$ tends to $1/2$ by dominated convergence, and the absolute tail is $e^(-N)N!/N^N$. Thus the ratio $K_(0,N)/a_0^N$ tends to a nonzero constant. Its squared modulus contributes a factor of one after the $N$th root.

The argument survives small offsets in the physical drive. For $0<h<=10^(-6)$ and $abs(d_i)<=h/100$, the previous bounds give $abs(z+1)<0.1$ at the dark endpoint and $abs(z)<=1/4$ at the bright endpoint. For the dark disk write $z=-1+xi$ and $eta=0.1$. On $[0,N]$ the absolute integrand is bounded by $exp(-(2-eta)t)$. On $t=N+s$ the absolute tail is at most
$ e^(-N)(1+eta)^N exp(N eta/(1+eta)) N!/N^N. $
It decays exponentially by Stirling's formula. The dark integral therefore tends uniformly to $1/(1-z)$. For the bright endpoint the coefficients $(N)_k/N^k$ are bounded by one and tend to one at each fixed $k$, so geometric domination gives the same nonzero limit there.

It follows that the two rooted weights are $abs(a_(j,Omega))^2$. They already sum to one by unitarity of the shifted two-mode evolution. At fixed positive $h$ in this range, taking the replica limit before the weak-gravity limit gives
$ P_"CWL"(j) = P_"SN"(j) = abs(a_(j,Omega))^2, quad j=0,1. $
The change from Ref. [1] concerns what is zero at ideal calibration. If both the detuning and pulse area are calibrated to a perfect shifted-mode swap, its dark amplitude vanishes and the rank-one collective matrix produces the factorial suppression in Eq. (122). For a pulse calibrated to the ordinary reference at the same laser drive, the collective dark amplitude vanishes instead. The relative amplitude then survives the replica root. Equation (103) in Ref. [1] was obtained at laser detuning $-Omega$; here it is $-omega$. The ratio of one refers specifically to this bare-reference protocol. At an ideal shifted-mode swap the centered SN dark amplitude is zero, while the CWL dark weight can receive a collective contribution. A measured-resonance retuning alone therefore supplies no general SN/CWL ratio.
#pagebreak()
= Scope of the optical prediction
For the specified first swap, the common-drive calculation gives an SN/CWL dark-count ratio of one. At the nominal area each probability equals $pi^2 epsilon^4/4+O(epsilon^6)$. The equality holds for the two exclusive probabilities in the stated rotating-wave model. At $h=10^(-6)$ the interval allows only $10^(-8)$ rad of area error per pulse. Predictions for a physical apparatus additionally require the terms discarded in arriving at a one-excitation model.

An ordinary-quantum example shows the scale of this issue. Keep the same pulse settings and restore $g_*(a+a^dagger)(c+c^dagger)$ in the quadratic Hamiltonian. At zero gravity the free hold rotates both modes through $10pi$, so the two pulses concatenate to time $t=pi/g_*$. The symmetric and antisymmetric optical–mechanical coordinates have frequencies $omega_+ = sqrt(omega(omega+2g_*))$ and $omega_- = sqrt(omega(omega-2g_*))$. Their Bogoliubov coefficients are
$ u_± = cos(omega_± t)-i (omega ± g_*)/(omega_±) sin(omega_± t). $
Even the single final state with zero photons and one phonon contributes the probability
$ B = abs(1/u_+^* - 1/u_-^*)^2/(4 abs(u_+ u_-)) approx 1.56738 times 10^(-5). $
To obtain this expression, write the input and output as the sum and difference of the two one-quantum normal-mode states. Each mode preserves parity. For each mode the ratio of the one-particle and vacuum diagonal amplitudes is $1/u^*$, and the vacuum probability is $1/abs(u)$. These give the expression above. Its value is strictly positive: the phase angles are $pi sqrt(440)$ and $pi sqrt(360)$, whose cosines differ because neither the sum nor the difference of the two square roots is an even integer.

This is a lower bound on an ordinary zero-count probability at fixed drive strength. At $h=10^(-6)$ the nominal rotating-wave gravity rate is about $2.47 times 10^(-12)$. A subtraction could remove a known constant background. In the full centered SN oscillator, changing $h$ also changes the pulse response. For these settings, differentiation of the inclusive quadratic zero-count probability gives a slope of $-0.012624$ at $h=0$ (see the accompanying slope derivation and verification script). This derived slope concerns SN alone. A common constant subtraction leaves this linear term. To infer a physical coefficient, however, the subtraction would have to control the difference of probabilities at order $o(h^2)$ and include all mechanical final states compatible with zero optical photons. Pair creation opens additional outcomes, outside the two exclusive endpoints used in the passive calculation. The calculation here establishes a change to the approximate comparison in Ref. [1]; the full experimental ratio remains open.

*Data availability.* This calculation uses no experimental data. #link("https://github.com/Quantyra/cwl-common-drive-comment/tree/review/three-lens-v1.0.0")[Source, scripts and review reports].

*Use of AI.* OpenAI Codex (GPT-6.2-Astra, xhigh reasoning) assisted with source comparison, mathematical derivation, verification code and manuscript preparation. The author specified the common-drive question, the source equations and the decision criteria. Grok 4.7, Claude Opus 5 and Antigravity (configured Gemini 3.8 Flash High) reviewed the preprint. Their reports informed the calibration analysis and the background check. The accompanying AI-assisted scripts reproduce the pulse propagator, finite-replica amplitudes and centered SN slope. Final human verification for journal submission is pending.

